@lang en
IMPORT prelude

-- California interpretation provisions: the general rules of construction that have a computable answer.
--
-- Sources: Gov. Code §§ 7, 8, 14, 16, 16.5, 18, 9612; Civ. Code §§ 12, 14(a), 17, 18, 19; Code Civ. Proc. §§ 11, 15, 17(b), 34.
-- The quotation lines are copied by script from the converter-made plain-text rendering of the sections (not the official text)
-- and checked against it by srcrefs.py.
--
-- Each rule is a function of facts the caller supplies as inputs.  A case the text does not answer is a named REFUSE, never a default.
-- The deposit has NO general rule that deems a document served or a notice given on some day after it is sent: the two functions
-- at the end of this file refuse, and say why.  Target: unstable (REFUSE).  Imports: prelude only.

§ `Shall and may`

DECLARE `Word of the text` IS ONE OF
    shall
    may

DECLARE `Force of a word` IS ONE OF
    mandatory
    permissive

-- Gov. Code § 14: "'Shall' is mandatory and 'may' is permissive."
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:57 | 14. “Shall” is mandatory and “may” is permissive.
@ref Gov. Code § 14 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:57)
GIVEN word IS A `Word of the text`
GIVETH A `Force of a word`
`the force of the word in the Government Code` word MEANS
    CONSIDER word
    WHEN shall THEN mandatory
    WHEN may THEN permissive

§ `Authority, deputies and officers acting together`

DECLARE `Who exercises a power of a public officer` IS ONE OF
    `the officer`
    `a deputy of the officer`
    `a person the officer authorized pursuant to law`
    `a person the officer authorized, not pursuant to law`
    `another person`

-- Gov. Code § 7: "Whenever a power is granted to, or a duty is imposed upon, a public officer, the power may be exercised or the duty may be
-- performed by a deputy of the officer or by a person authorized, pursuant to law, by the officer, unless this code expressly provides otherwise."
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:20 | 7. Whenever a power is granted to, or a duty is imposed upon, a public officer, the power may be exercised or the duty may be performed by a deputy of the officer or by a person authorized, pursuant to law, by the officer, unless this code expressly provides otherwise.
@ref Gov. Code § 7 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:20)
GIVEN who IS A `Who exercises a power of a public officer`
      `this code expressly provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a power of a public officer may be exercised, or a duty performed, by` who `given that` `this code expressly provides otherwise` MEANS
    CONSIDER who
    WHEN `the officer` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a deputy of the officer` THEN NOT `this code expressly provides otherwise`
    WHEN `a person the officer authorized pursuant to law` THEN NOT `this code expressly provides otherwise`
    WHEN `a person the officer authorized, not pursuant to law` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 7 lets a deputy or a person authorized pursuant to law exercise the power; it does not say whether a person the officer authorized otherwise than pursuant to law may"
    WHEN `another person` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 7 lets a deputy or a person authorized pursuant to law exercise the power; it does not say that no one else may"

-- Civ. Code § 12 and Code Civ. Proc. § 15: "Words giving a joint authority to three or more public officers or other persons are construed as
-- giving such authority to a majority of them, unless it is otherwise expressed in the Act giving the authority."  A majority is more than half.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:53 | 12. Words giving a joint authority to three or more public officers or other persons are construed as giving such authority to a majority of them, unless it is otherwise expressed in the Act giving the authority.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:54 | 15. Words giving a joint authority to three or more public officers or other persons are construed as giving such authority to a majority of them, unless it is otherwise expressed in the Act giving the authority.
@ref Civ. Code § 12, Code Civ. Proc. § 15 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:53, src:CCP-ss-1-34:54)
GIVEN `the number of officers or persons holding the joint authority` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of them who act` IS A NUMBER
      `the Act giving the authority expresses otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a joint authority may be exercised by those acting` `the number of officers or persons holding the joint authority` `the number of them who act` `given that` `the Act giving the authority expresses otherwise` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the Act giving the authority expresses otherwise`
            THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 12 and Code Civ. Proc. § 15 yield to what the Act giving the authority expresses; the Act decides"
        IF `the number of officers or persons holding the joint authority` LESS THAN 3
            THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 12 and Code Civ. Proc. § 15 speak of a joint authority given to three or more officers or persons"
        IF `the number of them who act` LESS THAN 0 OR `the number of them who act` GREATER THAN `the number of officers or persons holding the joint authority`
            THEN REFUSE "the number acting cannot be negative or exceed the number holding the authority"
        OTHERWISE 2 TIMES `the number of them who act` GREATER THAN `the number of officers or persons holding the joint authority`

§ `Writing, signature and electronic signature`

-- Gov. Code § 8: "Writing includes any form of recorded message capable of comprehension by ordinary visual means."
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:41 | 8. Writing includes any form of recorded message capable of comprehension by ordinary visual means. Whenever any notice, report, statement, or record is required or authorized by this code, it shall be made in writing in the English language unless it is expressly provided otherwise.
@ref Gov. Code § 8 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:41)
GIVEN `the message is recorded` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is capable of comprehension by ordinary visual means` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a message is a writing` `the message is recorded` `it is capable of comprehension by ordinary visual means` MEANS
    `the message is recorded` AND `it is capable of comprehension by ordinary visual means`

-- § 8, second sentence: a notice, report, statement or record required or authorized by the code "shall be made in writing in the English
-- language unless it is expressly provided otherwise."  The text says what the code requires, not which version of a document in two
-- languages prevails (fork F-language in NOTES): that question is refused.
@ref Gov. Code § 8 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:41)
GIVEN `it is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is in the English language` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is expressly provided otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a notice, report, statement or record required or authorized by the Government Code is made as the code requires` `it is in writing` `it is in the English language` `it is expressly provided otherwise` MEANS
    IF `it is expressly provided otherwise`
    THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 8 yields to what is expressly provided otherwise; the provision that does so decides the form"
    ELSE `it is in writing` AND `it is in the English language`

@ref Gov. Code § 8 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:41)
GIVETH A STRING
`the version of a document in two languages that has authority` MEANS
    REFUSE "Gov. Code § 8 requires a notice, report, statement or record to be in English; no deposited section says which of two language versions of a document has authority"

-- Gov. Code § 16, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(11): a signature includes a mark "when the signer ... can not write, such signer's
-- ... name being written near the mark by a witness who writes his own name near the signer's ... name".
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:61 | 16. “Signature” or “subscription” includes mark when the signer or subscriber can not write, such signer’s or subscriber’s name being written near the mark by a witness who writes his own name near the signer’s or subscriber’s name; but a signature or subscription by mark can be acknowledged or can serve as a signature or subscription to a sworn statement only when two witnesses so sign their own names thereto.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57 | 14. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present; words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter; the singular number includes the plural, and the plural the singular; the word person includes a corporation as well as a natural person; county includes city and county; writing includes printing and typewriting; oath includes affirmation or
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:58 | declaration; and every mode of oral statement, under oath or affirmation, is embraced by the term “testify,” and every written one in the term “depose”; signature or subscription includes mark, when the person cannot write, his name being written near it, by a person who writes his own name as a witness; provided, that when a signature is by mark it must in order that the same may be acknowledged or may serve as the signature to any sworn statement be witnessed by two persons who must subscribe their own names as witnesses thereto.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:58 | 17. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present. Words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter. The singular number includes the plural and the plural number includes the singular.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:59 | (b) As used in this code, the following words have the following meanings, unless otherwise apparent
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:60 | from the context:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:61 | (1) “Affinity” signifies the connection existing in consequence of marriage, between each of the married persons and the blood relatives of the other when applied to the marriage relation.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:62 | (2) “County” includes “city and county.”
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:63 | (3) “Electronic signature” means an electronic sound, symbol, or process attached to or logically associated with an electronic record and executed or adopted by a person with the intent to sign the electronic record.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:64 | (4) “Month” means a calendar month, unless otherwise expressed.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:65 | (5) “Oath” includes an affirmation or declaration.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:66 | (A) “Depose” includes any written statement made
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:67 | under oath or affirmation.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:68 | (B) “Testify” includes any mode of oral statement made under oath or affirmation.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:69 | (6) “Person” includes a corporation as well as a natural person.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:70 | (7) “Process” signifies a writ or summons issued in the course of a judicial proceeding.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:71 | (8) “Property” includes both personal and real property.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:72 | (A) “Personal property” includes money, goods, chattels, things in action, and evidences of debt.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:73 | (B) “Real property” is coextensive with lands, tenements, and hereditaments.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:74 | (9) “Section” refers to a section of this code, unless some other code or
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:75 | statute is expressly mentioned.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:76 | (10) “Sheriff” includes marshal.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:77 | (11) “Signature” or “subscription” includes a mark of a person’s name, if the person cannot write, with his or her name being written near it by a person who writes his or her own name as a witness. In order that a mark may be acknowledged or serve as the signature to any sworn statement, it shall be witnessed by two persons who shall subscribe their own names as witnesses thereto.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:78 | (12) “Spouse” includes “registered domestic partner,” as required by Section 297.5 of the Family Code.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:79 | (13) “State” includes the District of Columbia and the territories when applied to the different parts of the United States, and the words “United States” may include the district and territories.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:80 | (14) “Will” includes codicil.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:81 | (15) “Writ” means an order or precept in writing, issued in the name of the people, or of a court or judicial officer.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:82 | (16) “Writing” includes printing and typewriting.
@ref Gov. Code § 16, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(11) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:61, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57-58, src:CCP-ss-1-34:58-82)
GIVEN `the signer cannot write` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the signer's name is written near the mark by a witness` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that witness writes his or her own name near the signer's name` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a mark is a signature or subscription` `the signer cannot write` `the signer's name is written near the mark by a witness` `that witness writes his or her own name near the signer's name` MEANS
    `the signer cannot write` AND `the signer's name is written near the mark by a witness` AND `that witness writes his or her own name near the signer's name`

-- The same sections: a signature by mark "can be acknowledged or can serve as a signature ... to a sworn statement only when two witnesses so
-- sign their own names thereto."
@ref Gov. Code § 16, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(11) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:61, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57-58, src:CCP-ss-1-34:58-82)
GIVEN `the mark is a signature or subscription` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the number of witnesses who sign their own names` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a signature by mark can be acknowledged or serve as the signature to a sworn statement` `the mark is a signature or subscription` `the number of witnesses who sign their own names` MEANS
    `the mark is a signature or subscription` AND `the number of witnesses who sign their own names` AT LEAST 2

-- Gov. Code § 16.5(a): a digital signature of a party to a communication with a public entity that elects to use it "shall have the same force
-- and effect as the use of a manual signature if and only if it embodies all of the following attributes": unique to the person; capable of
-- verification; under the person's sole control; linked to data so that a change to the data invalidates it; conforms to regulations of the
-- Secretary of State.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:63 | 16.5. (a) In any written communication with a public entity, as defined in Section 811.2, in which a signature is required or used, any party to the communication may affix a signature by use of a digital signature that complies with the requirements of this section. If a public entity elects to use a digital signature, that digital signature shall have the same force and effect as the use of a manual signature if and only if it embodies all of the following attributes:
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:64 | (1) It is unique to the person using it.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:65 | (2) It is capable of verification.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:66 | (3) It is under the sole control of the person using it.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:67 | (4) It is linked to data in such a manner that if the data are changed, the digital signature is invalidated.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:68 | (5) It conforms to regulations adopted by the Secretary of State. Initial regulations shall be adopted no later than January 1, 1997. In developing these regulations, the secretary shall seek the advice of public and private entities, including, but not limited to, the Department of Information Technology, the California Environmental Protection Agency, and the Department of General Services. Before the secretary adopts the regulations, he or she shall hold at least one public hearing to receive comments.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:69 | (b) The use or acceptance of a digital signature shall
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:70 | be at the option of the parties. Nothing in this section shall require a public entity to use or permit the use of a digital signature.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:71 | (c) Digital signatures employed pursuant to Section 71066 of the Public Resources Code are exempted from this section.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:72 | (d) “Digital signature” means an electronic identifier, created by computer, intended by the party using it to have the same force and effect as the use of a manual signature. For purposes of this section, a digital signature is a type of “electronic signature” as defined in subdivision (h) of Section 1633.2 of the Civil Code.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:73 | (e) Nothing in this section shall limit the right of a public entity or government agency to use and accept an “electronic signature” as defined in subdivision (h) of Section 1633.2 of the Civil Code.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:74 | (f) Regulations adopted by the Secretary of State to implement this section apply only to a public entity’s use of a “digital signature” and not to use of any other type of “electronic signature” authorized in the Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (Title 2.5 (commencing with Section 1633.1) of Part 2 of Division 3 of the Civil Code).
@ref Gov. Code § 16.5(a) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:63-74)
GIVEN `the public entity elects to use a digital signature` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is unique to the person using it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is capable of verification` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is under the sole control of the person using it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is linked to data so that a change to the data invalidates it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it conforms to the regulations of the Secretary of State` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a digital signature has the same force and effect as a manual signature` `the public entity elects to use a digital signature` `it is unique to the person using it` `it is capable of verification` `it is under the sole control of the person using it` `it is linked to data so that a change to the data invalidates it` `it conforms to the regulations of the Secretary of State` MEANS
        `the public entity elects to use a digital signature`
    AND `it is unique to the person using it`
    AND `it is capable of verification`
    AND `it is under the sole control of the person using it`
    AND `it is linked to data so that a change to the data invalidates it`
    AND `it conforms to the regulations of the Secretary of State`

-- § 16.5(b): "Nothing in this section shall require a public entity to use or permit the use of a digital signature."
@ref Gov. Code § 16.5(b) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:63-74)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a public entity is required to use or permit the use of a digital signature` MEANS FALSE

-- § 16.5(c): digital signatures employed under section 71066 of the Public Resources Code "are exempted from this section".
@ref Gov. Code § 16.5(c) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:63-74)
GIVEN `it is employed pursuant to section 71066 of the Public Resources Code` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 16.5 applies to the digital signature` `it is employed pursuant to section 71066 of the Public Resources Code` MEANS
    NOT `it is employed pursuant to section 71066 of the Public Resources Code`

-- Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(3): an electronic signature "means an electronic sound, symbol, or process attached to or logically associated with an
-- electronic record and executed or adopted by a person with the intent to sign the electronic record."
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:58 | 17. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present. Words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter. The singular number includes the plural and the plural number includes the singular.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:59 | (b) As used in this code, the following words have the following meanings, unless otherwise apparent
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:60 | from the context:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:61 | (1) “Affinity” signifies the connection existing in consequence of marriage, between each of the married persons and the blood relatives of the other when applied to the marriage relation.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:62 | (2) “County” includes “city and county.”
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:63 | (3) “Electronic signature” means an electronic sound, symbol, or process attached to or logically associated with an electronic record and executed or adopted by a person with the intent to sign the electronic record.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:64 | (4) “Month” means a calendar month, unless otherwise expressed.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:65 | (5) “Oath” includes an affirmation or declaration.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:66 | (A) “Depose” includes any written statement made
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:67 | under oath or affirmation.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:68 | (B) “Testify” includes any mode of oral statement made under oath or affirmation.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:69 | (6) “Person” includes a corporation as well as a natural person.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:70 | (7) “Process” signifies a writ or summons issued in the course of a judicial proceeding.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:71 | (8) “Property” includes both personal and real property.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:72 | (A) “Personal property” includes money, goods, chattels, things in action, and evidences of debt.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:73 | (B) “Real property” is coextensive with lands, tenements, and hereditaments.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:74 | (9) “Section” refers to a section of this code, unless some other code or
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:75 | statute is expressly mentioned.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:76 | (10) “Sheriff” includes marshal.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:77 | (11) “Signature” or “subscription” includes a mark of a person’s name, if the person cannot write, with his or her name being written near it by a person who writes his or her own name as a witness. In order that a mark may be acknowledged or serve as the signature to any sworn statement, it shall be witnessed by two persons who shall subscribe their own names as witnesses thereto.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:78 | (12) “Spouse” includes “registered domestic partner,” as required by Section 297.5 of the Family Code.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:79 | (13) “State” includes the District of Columbia and the territories when applied to the different parts of the United States, and the words “United States” may include the district and territories.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:80 | (14) “Will” includes codicil.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:81 | (15) “Writ” means an order or precept in writing, issued in the name of the people, or of a court or judicial officer.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:82 | (16) “Writing” includes printing and typewriting.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(3) (src:CCP-ss-1-34:58-82)
GIVEN `it is an electronic sound, symbol or process` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is attached to or logically associated with an electronic record` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is executed or adopted by a person with the intent to sign the record` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a thing is an electronic signature` `it is an electronic sound, symbol or process` `it is attached to or logically associated with an electronic record` `it is executed or adopted by a person with the intent to sign the record` MEANS
    `it is an electronic sound, symbol or process` AND `it is attached to or logically associated with an electronic record` AND `it is executed or adopted by a person with the intent to sign the record`

-- Code Civ. Proc. § 34: "An electronic signature, as defined in Section 17, by a court or judicial officer shall be as effective as an original
-- signature."  The section speaks of a court or judicial officer only.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:128 | 34. An electronic signature, as defined in Section 17, by a court or judicial officer shall be as effective as an original signature.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 34 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:128)
GIVEN `the signer is a court or judicial officer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the thing is an electronic signature` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an electronic signature is as effective as an original signature under Code Civ. Proc. § 34` `the signer is a court or judicial officer` `the thing is an electronic signature` MEANS
    IF `the signer is a court or judicial officer`
    THEN `the thing is an electronic signature`
    ELSE REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 34 makes an electronic signature by a court or judicial officer as effective as an original signature; it does not say the same of anyone else's"

§ `Mail, service and notice`

DECLARE `Method of mailing` IS ONE OF
    `registered mail`
    `certified mail`
    `another method of mailing`

-- Civ. Code § 17 and Code Civ. Proc. § 11: "Wherever any notice or other communication is required by this code to be mailed by registered mail
-- ..., the mailing of such notice or other communication by certified mail shall be deemed to be a sufficient compliance with the requirements of law."
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:69 | 17. Wherever any notice or other communication is required by this code to be mailed by registered mail, the mailing of such notice or other communication by certified mail shall be deemed to be a sufficient compliance with the requirements of law.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:32 | 11. Wherever any notice or other communication is required by this code to be mailed by registered mail by or to any person or corporation, the mailing of such notice or other communication by certified mail shall be deemed to be a sufficient compliance with the requirements of law.
@ref Civ. Code § 17, Code Civ. Proc. § 11 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:69, src:CCP-ss-1-34:32)
GIVEN method IS A `Method of mailing`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a requirement of the Civil Code or the Code of Civil Procedure that a notice be mailed by registered mail is met by` method MEANS
    CONSIDER method
    WHEN `registered mail` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `certified mail` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another method of mailing` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 17 and Code Civ. Proc. § 11 say that certified mail is a sufficient compliance where registered mail is required; no deposited section says whether any other method is"

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:42 | 12c. (a) Where any law requires an act to be performed no later than a specified number of days before a hearing date, the last day to perform that act shall be determined by counting backward from the hearing date, excluding the day of the hearing as provided by Section 12.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:43 | (b) Any additional days added to the specified number of days because of a particular method of service shall be computed by counting
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:44 | backward from the day determined in accordance with subdivision (a).
-- THE DEPOSIT HAS NO GENERAL RULE THAT DEEMS SERVICE MADE OR NOTICE GIVEN ON SOME DAY AFTER MAILING.  Civ. Code § 17 and Code Civ. Proc. § 11
-- are about registered and certified mail only; Code Civ. Proc. § 12c(b) counts "additional days added ... because of a particular method of service"
-- backward from a hearing, and says nothing about when service is deemed made.  These functions exist so that the question has a named answer.
@ref Civ. Code § 17, Code Civ. Proc. § 11, § 12c (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:69, src:CCP-ss-1-34:32, src:CCP-ss-1-34:42-44)
GIVEN `the day the document is mailed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day service by mail is deemed made, the document having been mailed on` `the day the document is mailed` MEANS
    REFUSE "no deposited section deems service by mail made on any day: Civ. Code § 17 and Code Civ. Proc. § 11 say only that certified mail is sufficient where registered mail is required, and Code Civ. Proc. § 12c(b) counts method-of-service days backward from a hearing"

@ref Civ. Code § 17, Code Civ. Proc. § 11, § 12c (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:69, src:CCP-ss-1-34:32, src:CCP-ss-1-34:42-44)
GIVEN `the day the notice is sent` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day a notice is deemed given, the notice having been sent on` `the day the notice is sent` MEANS
    REFUSE "no deposited section deems a notice given on any day after it is sent: Civ. Code §§ 18 and 19 define actual and constructive notice, and say nothing of the day a notice sent is given"

-- Civ. Code § 18: "Notice is: 1. Actual -- which consists in express information of a fact; or, 2. Constructive -- which is imputed by law."
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:71 | 18. Notice is:
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:72 | 1. Actual—which consists in express information of a fact; or,
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:73 | 2. Constructive—which is imputed by law.
@ref Civ. Code § 18 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:71-73)
GIVEN `the person has express information of the fact` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person has actual notice of a fact` `the person has express information of the fact` MEANS
    `the person has express information of the fact`

@ref Civ. Code § 18 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:71-73)
GIVEN `the law imputes the fact to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person has constructive notice of a fact` `the law imputes the fact to the person` MEANS
    `the law imputes the fact to the person`

-- Civ. Code § 19: "Every person who has actual notice of circumstances sufficient to put a prudent person upon inquiry as to a particular fact has
-- constructive notice of the fact itself in all cases in which, by prosecuting such inquiry, he or she might have learned that fact."
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:75 | 19. Every person who has actual notice of circumstances sufficient to put a prudent person upon inquiry as to a particular fact has constructive notice of the fact itself in all cases in which, by prosecuting such inquiry, he or she might have learned that fact.
@ref Civ. Code § 19 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:75)
GIVEN `the person has actual notice of circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `those circumstances are sufficient to put a prudent person upon inquiry as to the fact` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by prosecuting the inquiry the person might have learned the fact` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person has constructive notice of the fact itself under Civ. Code § 19` `the person has actual notice of circumstances` `those circumstances are sufficient to put a prudent person upon inquiry as to the fact` `by prosecuting the inquiry the person might have learned the fact` MEANS
    `the person has actual notice of circumstances` AND `those circumstances are sufficient to put a prudent person upon inquiry as to the fact` AND `by prosecuting the inquiry the person might have learned the fact`

§ `Words and phrases`

DECLARE `Meaning of State` IS ONE OF
    `the State of California`
    `the part of the United States, including the District of Columbia and the territories`

-- Gov. Code § 18: "'State' means the State of California, unless applied to the different parts of the United States.  In the latter case, it
-- includes the District of Columbia and the territories."
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:78 | 18. “State” means the State of California, unless applied to the different parts of the United States. In the latter case, it includes the District of Columbia and the territories.
@ref Gov. Code § 18 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:78)
GIVEN `the word is applied to the different parts of the United States` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Meaning of State`
`the meaning of State in the Government Code` `the word is applied to the different parts of the United States` MEANS
    IF `the word is applied to the different parts of the United States`
    THEN `the part of the United States, including the District of Columbia and the territories`
    ELSE `the State of California`

DECLARE `Term of the military service` IS ONE OF
    `United States Army`
    `Army of the United States`
    `United States Navy`
    `military service`
    `another term`

-- Gov. Code § 9612: "Unless the context otherwise requires whenever the terms United States Army, Army of the United States, United States Navy,
-- or military service appear in a statute whether singly or any combination of them, they shall be deemed to include the United States Air Force."
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:38 | 9612. Unless the context otherwise requires whenever the terms United States Army, Army of the United States, United States Navy, or military service appear in a statute whether singly or any combination of them, they shall be deemed to include the United States Air Force.
@ref Gov. Code § 9612 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:38)
GIVEN term IS A `Term of the military service`
      `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a term in a statute is deemed to include the United States Air Force` term `given that` `the context otherwise requires` MEANS
    CONSIDER term
    WHEN `United States Army` THEN NOT `the context otherwise requires`
    WHEN `Army of the United States` THEN NOT `the context otherwise requires`
    WHEN `United States Navy` THEN NOT `the context otherwise requires`
    WHEN `military service` THEN NOT `the context otherwise requires`
    WHEN `another term` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9612 names four terms; it does not say whether any other term includes the United States Air Force"
